00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.080
Okay. So I will be um so the focus of my

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:32.400
talk is really cellular metabolism and

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:35.199
what is metabolism? Metabolism is the

00:07:35.199 --> 00:07:37.919
sum of all chemical reactions within a

00:07:37.919 --> 00:07:40.960
biological system. And this is a map

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:42.800
showing kind of the central metabolic

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:45.440
pathways in mamlian cells. And I know it

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:46.960
looks quite complicated, but this is

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.919
actually a gross oversimplification of

00:07:49.919 --> 00:07:51.759
metabolism.

00:07:51.759 --> 00:07:54.639
So we know that there&#x27;s over 220,000

00:07:54.639 --> 00:07:57.919
reported human metabolites in humans.

00:07:57.919 --> 00:08:00.080
And just in human serum for example,

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:03.520
there&#x27;s over 4,500 serum metabolites.

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:05.440
And so one of the questions that that

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:08.479
that I and so many people for centuries

00:08:08.479 --> 00:08:10.400
and millennia have been interested in is

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:13.039
how is metabolism regulated and

00:08:13.039 --> 00:08:15.440
controlled? And how do humans and living

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:18.720
organisms maintain metabolism to sustain

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:22.000
life? And so Aristotle became started

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:23.680
thinking about this in the 4th century

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:25.840
BC. and he made the observation that the

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:28.800
food that we eat must be what provides

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:30.720
the energy and the building blocks that

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:33.599
we need to grow and sustain life in uh

00:08:33.599 --> 00:08:37.599
humans. And a couple centuries later,

00:08:37.599 --> 00:08:41.360
Santorius of Padawa um actually expanded

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:43.360
upon this and took very detailed

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:46.080
measurements of his consumption and

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:48.399
excretion over 30 years and made the

00:08:48.399 --> 00:08:51.200
observation that for every 8 pounds of

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:54.480
food he ate only 3 lb of waste was consu

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:56.480
was produced. So therefore he said this

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:00.560
must be going somewhere and um uh that

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:02.320
principle has really been expanded at

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:04.160
the cellular level. So in the 19th

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:06.399
century, Ernest Overbertton

00:09:06.399 --> 00:09:09.120
studied how metabolites get in and out

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:12.399
of cells and how they um are transported

00:09:12.399 --> 00:09:14.000
into the cell and how that enables them

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:15.600
to be used for different cellular

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:18.320
metabolic pathways and processes.

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:21.200
And so really the key player of cellular

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:23.680
metabolism is transporters. And

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:26.000
transporters are protein proteins that

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.240
sit on the cell membrane that bring

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:30.480
nutrients in and out of the cells. And

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:32.720
this is a really fundamental process for

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:35.279
cellular biology and organismal biology

00:09:35.279 --> 00:09:38.320
in general. And the fact that this is so

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:40.000
important is highlighted by the fact

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:42.720
that over 10% of the human genome, all

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:45.519
genes that humans encode, they encode

00:09:45.519 --> 00:09:47.200
for proteins that have transport

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:50.240
function. So it&#x27;s a very key function of

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:51.920
cellular homeostasis, but also

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:55.279
organismal homeostasis.

00:09:55.279 --> 00:09:58.000
And one of the largest families of these

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.279
um uh these uh solute carriers are or is

00:10:01.279 --> 00:10:02.800
known as the solute carrier super

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:05.920
family, the SLC&#x27;s. And there&#x27;s over 450

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:09.279
of these different um transporters which

00:10:09.279 --> 00:10:11.040
range in diversity of where they&#x27;re

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:12.720
located within the cell, what they

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:14.480
transport, what affinity they have for

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:16.399
their substrates.

00:10:16.399 --> 00:10:19.519
And um throughout evolution, these

00:10:19.519 --> 00:10:21.519
different transporters have evolved to

00:10:21.519 --> 00:10:24.160
enable organisms and cells to tightly

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:26.640
regulate their metabolism. So for

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:29.440
example, if we take glucose as an

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:30.800
example, which is a very abundant

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:32.800
metabolite, there&#x27;s at least four

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:35.040
different glucose transporters and they

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:36.640
change in where they are expressed in

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:39.360
the human body. So for example uh in the

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:41.040
brain you have this transporter called

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.120
glute 3 and that has very very high

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:45.279
affinity meaning it really really wants

00:10:45.279 --> 00:10:47.760
to transport glucose and that makes

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:50.079
sense because the brain has a high

00:10:50.079 --> 00:10:52.880
energetic demands. In other tissues such

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:57.839
as um in pancreatic beta cells, gl the

00:10:57.839 --> 00:11:01.519
affinity of uh the transporter glute 2

00:11:01.519 --> 00:11:03.360
has very low affinity for glucose and

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:05.360
that allows it to act as a sensor so

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:07.440
your pancreas can understand whether or

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:10.480
not you&#x27;ve recently fed and and induced

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:13.760
the types of organismal u metabolism

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:16.000
that your body needs to regulate uh

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:19.279
glucose levels. And so what do we know

00:11:19.279 --> 00:11:21.279
about other transporters other than

00:11:21.279 --> 00:11:24.079
glucose? Well, surprisingly very little.

00:11:24.079 --> 00:11:27.040
So of the 456 transporters that I told

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:29.200
you about, if we just survey them and

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:32.160
ask what what is transported by that

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:35.360
transporter, almost half of them, we

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:37.360
have very little evidence for what it

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:39.120
transports. About a quarter of them, we

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:41.120
have absolutely no idea whatsoever what

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:42.959
it transports. And if we look on the

00:11:42.959 --> 00:11:44.959
opposite end where we consider gold

00:11:44.959 --> 00:11:47.440
standard proof where we have a structure

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:49.920
of of the protein bound to its substrate

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:52.240
and cellular and biochemical evidence of

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:54.640
what it does, that&#x27;s only 5% of this

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:56.240
whole family of transporters. And I told

00:11:56.240 --> 00:11:57.760
you this is the biggest and most

00:11:57.760 --> 00:11:59.600
important family of metabolite

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:02.880
transporters in humans. And the fact

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:05.920
that we know so little is is um even

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:07.680
more striking when you have take into

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:10.320
account that over 50% of SLC

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:12.079
transporters are associated with human

00:12:12.079 --> 00:12:13.920
disease.

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:16.160
And so when I started my postto and some

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:17.360
of the work that I&#x27;m doing in my

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:21.680
laboratory now is can we systematically

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:24.160
identify the physiological substrates of

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:26.160
metabolite transporters and by doing

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:28.079
that understand biology and understand

00:12:28.079 --> 00:12:32.160
potentially human disease. So we took

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:34.399
this G-W was based approach. So G-W was

00:12:34.399 --> 00:12:36.639
genomewide association studies. So they

00:12:36.639 --> 00:12:39.279
are studies in which they you look at

00:12:39.279 --> 00:12:42.160
the different snips or varants in the

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:44.560
genome of people and then you can

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:46.240
associate that with phenotypes or

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:48.959
clinical outcomes that those patients

00:12:48.959 --> 00:12:51.440
have. And so we took this data set for

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:55.440
which they had both the um the the

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:57.440
genetic information for 6,000 people,

00:12:57.440 --> 00:12:59.680
but they also measured serum metabolite

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:01.279
levels for these patients. And we

00:13:01.279 --> 00:13:02.800
thought we could use this as a discovery

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:05.200
platform to try to identify associations

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:08.000
genetically and statistically between

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:10.639
metabolites in circulating serum and

00:13:10.639 --> 00:13:12.800
potential variance in those trans in

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:15.120
potential transporters in people and

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:18.639
kind of use human biology as a way as a

00:13:18.639 --> 00:13:20.000
discovery platform to kind of

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:23.200
deorphanize these transporters. And so

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:25.120
normally when people do G-W was they&#x27;re

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:27.040
looking for genetic variations that are

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:30.399
associated with human disease. And what

00:13:30.399 --> 00:13:32.000
we&#x27;re trying to do here is the opposite

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:33.680
where we&#x27;re actually looking for

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:36.880
associations in uh the circulating

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:39.200
levels of metabolites of these patients

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:42.160
with variance in potential uh uh

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:45.040
transporters to try to deorganize who is

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:48.320
transporting what in an unbiased way.

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:50.639
And so the idea of looking at

00:13:50.639 --> 00:13:52.880
differences in serum metabolites to

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:55.920
inform uh physiology is not new. So

00:13:55.920 --> 00:13:58.480
there are a number of metabolites for

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:00.399
which there&#x27;s human diseases caused by

00:14:00.399 --> 00:14:03.040
elevated levels of metabolites. So this

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:05.600
isn&#x27;t a a foreign concept, but it was a

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:07.680
a unique approach that we took to to

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:10.399
deorganize these transporters.

00:14:10.399 --> 00:14:13.199
And we we first were able to show that

00:14:13.199 --> 00:14:14.560
things that you would expect from the

00:14:14.560 --> 00:14:15.920
literature that you could pick up. So

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:17.360
for example, cholesterol is a very

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:19.199
important metabolite and the transporter

00:14:19.199 --> 00:14:21.519
that takes up cholesterol is LDLR. And

00:14:21.519 --> 00:14:23.199
we were able to find that variance in

00:14:23.199 --> 00:14:26.320
this transporter are associated with uh

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:29.120
changes in serum levels of that metaph.

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:31.040
And so we next turned our attention to

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:32.480
what was unknown, what&#x27;s previously

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:34.480
unreported. And the one transport that

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:35.600
I&#x27;m going to tell you about that I&#x27;ve

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:37.519
been working on is this transporter

00:14:37.519 --> 00:14:39.360
called FVCR1.

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:41.839
It&#x27;s a expressed on the plasma membrane

00:14:41.839 --> 00:14:44.000
of cells. And we found that it was

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:45.600
associated with this metabolite called

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:47.920
phospholine.

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:49.920
And we were interested in this metab in

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:52.240
this association because it was not only

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:54.560
associated with phosphocoline but also

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:56.000
the downstream metabolite of

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:58.399
phosphocoline phospatidylcholine which

00:14:58.399 --> 00:15:00.399
led us to believe that maybe this is a

00:15:00.399 --> 00:15:02.880
real bonafide hit that has something to

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:05.360
do with choline metabolism.

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:07.120
Okay, taking a step back. What is

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:09.040
choline? I&#x27;m sure most of you have

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:10.399
probably heard of it before, but it&#x27;s

00:15:10.399 --> 00:15:13.360
this fivecarbon essential nutrient that

00:15:13.360 --> 00:15:16.560
we have to take up from our diet. And it

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:19.120
has once choline goes into the cell it

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:21.360
has multiple metabolic fates of what it

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:23.680
can become. So probably most wellknown

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:25.839
is it can become acetylcholine and

00:15:25.839 --> 00:15:27.519
acetylcholine is a very important

00:15:27.519 --> 00:15:29.839
neurotransmitter.

00:15:29.839 --> 00:15:31.519
It can also be used to make

00:15:31.519 --> 00:15:33.279
phospatidylcholine which I just

00:15:33.279 --> 00:15:35.440
discussed before but phospatidylcholine

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:38.320
is the most abundant phospholipid in

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:40.320
mamalian membrane. So this is really

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:42.800
what makes a cell a cell this layer of

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:45.040
phospatidylcholine.

00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:47.120
Additionally, it can be used to make

00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:49.040
