00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:51.120
important in one carbon metabolism,

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:52.800
which plays a role in epigenetic

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:54.720
regulation and transcription and other

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:57.199
things that I won&#x27;t touch upon today.

00:15:57.199 --> 00:15:59.279
But what I want to emphasize is that we

00:15:59.279 --> 00:16:01.120
know and I&#x27;ll I&#x27;ll talk I&#x27;ll walk

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:02.639
through this in a second, but we know

00:16:02.639 --> 00:16:04.320
that choline must be obtained from the

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:06.160
diet and we know that it&#x27;s a pretty

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:09.920
abundant metabolite in human serum. And

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:11.440
so

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:15.360
the uh association of choline and um

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:17.120
physiology really came about in the

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:19.440
1930s. So Frederick Banting and Charles

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:22.959
Best were the first to show that if you

00:16:22.959 --> 00:16:24.800
have dogs that were uh had a

00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:26.880
pancreattomy. So they removed their

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:28.639
pancreas, they had fatty liver disease.

00:16:28.639 --> 00:16:30.240
And they found that if you gave these

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:32.959
dogs choline, it reversed the fatty

00:16:32.959 --> 00:16:35.040
liver that these dogs had. again showing

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:36.240
for the first time that there was an

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:38.480
association between the levels of a

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:40.639
given metabolite in the diet, choline in

00:16:40.639 --> 00:16:43.519
this case, and disease in an animal

00:16:43.519 --> 00:16:47.120
model. And really in the 1970s,

00:16:47.120 --> 00:16:49.199
before the 1970s, it really wasn&#x27;t even

00:16:49.199 --> 00:16:52.000
clear if choline was an essential

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:54.240
nutrient for humans. It was thought that

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:56.480
animals needed to eat choline, but

00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:58.079
humans didn&#x27;t need to eat choline. And

00:16:58.079 --> 00:16:59.680
plants, for example, can make their own

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:01.839
choline. But really, that came about in

00:17:01.839 --> 00:17:04.319
the 1970s. that dogma started to change

00:17:04.319 --> 00:17:08.000
and that really came about from studying

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:10.799
um nursing mothers and the contents of

00:17:10.799 --> 00:17:12.559
breast milk. So choline is very very

00:17:12.559 --> 00:17:14.799
high in breast milk and choline is

00:17:14.799 --> 00:17:16.959
passed from mothers through the placenta

00:17:16.959 --> 00:17:20.240
to developing adults and kind of that

00:17:20.240 --> 00:17:22.400
body of literature in the &#x27;9s finally

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:24.240
came to the conclusion and

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:25.839
recommendation from governmental bodies

00:17:25.839 --> 00:17:27.839
that choline is in fact an essential

00:17:27.839 --> 00:17:30.160
human nutrient that you have to take in

00:17:30.160 --> 00:17:32.640
your diet um in order to have normal

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:34.720
physiology and homeostasis. And they

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:37.360
really observed this for the first time

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:39.520
by doing studies in the 80s and 90s

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:41.679
where they gave people choline deficient

00:17:41.679 --> 00:17:43.280
diets and then asked what happened to

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:45.200
these people. And these people end up

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:47.280
developing fatty liver disease. They

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:48.880
have muscle wasting and they have liver

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:51.919
wasting liver uh degeneration.

00:17:51.919 --> 00:17:53.280
But one of the coolest things about

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:54.799
those studies is that when you

00:17:54.799 --> 00:17:56.799
reintroduce choline into the diet of

00:17:56.799 --> 00:17:58.320
people who if you&#x27;ve taken choline away

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:00.720
from those phenotypes and those diseases

00:18:00.720 --> 00:18:02.720
all go away. again showing this tight

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:04.960
link between uh the nutrients that you

00:18:04.960 --> 00:18:07.440
take up uh in your diet and your health

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:09.360
options.

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:11.679
Okay, so

00:18:11.679 --> 00:18:13.760
we know that choline because of its

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:15.679
structure must be transported into

00:18:15.679 --> 00:18:17.760
cells. It cannot just passively diffuse

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:21.039
across the membrane and get into cells.

00:18:21.039 --> 00:18:22.720
And going back to kind of the

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:24.240
association studies that I showed you

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:26.480
before, we hypothesized that maybe this

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:29.440
transporter that I discovered, FOVCR1,

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:32.080
is a transporter of choline and allows

00:18:32.080 --> 00:18:35.039
choline to get into cells. Okay, now

00:18:35.039 --> 00:18:37.360
getting into some actual science. How do

00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:39.200
we show this? So we can take cells and

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:40.799
genetically engineer them. So we knock

00:18:40.799 --> 00:18:43.120
out a gene and then add it back with an

00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:44.960
expression system. And we did this in

00:18:44.960 --> 00:18:47.280
cells. And then we just did metabolomics

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:49.039
where we can look at all metabolites

00:18:49.039 --> 00:18:50.799
detected in the cell and ask what goes

00:18:50.799 --> 00:18:53.360
up what goes down. And when we did that

00:18:53.360 --> 00:18:55.919
we found that in cells that don&#x27;t have

00:18:55.919 --> 00:18:58.640
ethylr1 this transporter they have very

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:01.039
low levels of phosphocoline this choline

00:19:01.039 --> 00:19:03.039
derived metabolite as well as choline

00:19:03.039 --> 00:19:04.799
and another choline derived metabolite

00:19:04.799 --> 00:19:06.640
beta.

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:08.080
And there&#x27;s another approach that we can

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:10.160
use especially in metabolomics which is

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:12.160
using carbon tracing. So we can take

00:19:12.160 --> 00:19:14.559
advantage of the fact that some carbons

00:19:14.559 --> 00:19:16.880
are naturally occurring to have uh

00:19:16.880 --> 00:19:18.799
they&#x27;re heavy naturally occurring. So we

00:19:18.799 --> 00:19:21.520
can feed these heavy carbons in a in a

00:19:21.520 --> 00:19:23.679
metabolite such as cold to cells and

00:19:23.679 --> 00:19:25.600
then trace how those carbons move

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:27.919
through the metabolism and ask where did

00:19:27.919 --> 00:19:30.080
that carbon go when we fed it to the

00:19:30.080 --> 00:19:31.919
cells. And so when we do that we see

00:19:31.919 --> 00:19:35.200
that cells without FLCR1 cannot

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:37.840
incorporate choline into the downstream

00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:40.240
metabolites of choline metabolism like

00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:43.120
phospholine in baby.

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:46.240
Okay. Now to really demonstrate that

00:19:46.240 --> 00:19:49.679
ethylvcr1 was a choline transporter we

00:19:49.679 --> 00:19:51.760
used radioactive choline and again

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:53.840
similar types of experiments gave it to

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:56.559
cells with and without ethylcr1. And the

00:19:56.559 --> 00:19:58.720
hypothesis being that if FLVCR1

00:19:58.720 --> 00:20:00.960
transports choline, you should need

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:03.919
